## How it works

A funnel keeps only the events you name and asks of each path: did it reach
these, in this order? Everything in between is ignored — which is what makes a
funnel easy to agree on with stakeholders, and unable to tell you why the drop
happened.

Two conventions worth being explicit about, because funnels in different tools
count differently:

- **Order matters, gaps do not.** A path counts at step N only if it already
  passed every earlier step, in sequence. Other events may happen in between,
  any number of times.
- **Paths are counted once.** The numbers are unique paths, not event
  occurrences — buying twice does not count twice.

The result carries two conversion rates per step, and mixing them up is the most
common misreading:

| Field | Denominator | Reads as |
|---|---|---|
| `conversion_rate` | all paths in the eventstream | "share of everyone who got this far" |
| `step_conversion_rate` | the previous step | "share of those who got here from the step before" |

Note the denominator of `conversion_rate`: *all* paths, including those that
never entered the funnel at all. If most of your eventstream never touches the
funnel, filter first ([`filter_paths`](/docs/data-processors/filter-paths)) so
the rate answers the question you meant.

### Confidence intervals and A/B tests

`ci_level=0.95` (or the **Confidence interval** selector in the sidebar: Off /
90% / 95% / 99%) puts sampling uncertainty on every number:

| Number | Interval | Test |
|---|---|---|
| a group's `conversion_rate` and `step_conversion_rate` | Wilson score | — |
| `delta_conversion_rate` (overall, per step) | Newcombe hybrid score | pooled two-proportion z-test, `delta_conversion_rate_p_value` |
| `delta_step_conversion_rate` (step to step) | Newcombe hybrid score | none — see below |

Only the **overall** deltas get a p-value. A step-to-step rate is conditional on
reaching the previous step, and in an experiment the treatment itself changes
who reaches it: a step delta compares two different populations, so it is
descriptive, never a treatment effect. Its interval is shown, marked † and
flagged `step_conditional`, but it carries no p-value and is never coloured.
The first step has no previous step, so its two deltas coincide.

The table colours a delta only when its interval excludes 0; everything else
stays grey, whatever its size. A funnel tests one comparison per step, so with
many steps some p-values fall under 0.05 by chance: `correction="holm"` (or
`"bonferroni"`, or `"bh"` for the false discovery rate) adjusts the overall-delta
p-values across the steps into `delta_conversion_rate_p_value_adjusted`. The
intervals are never adjusted, so a delta can be coloured while its adjusted
p-value is above your threshold — read the adjusted p-value for the decision.
The relative delta in the table divides by group 2, the control.

With intervals, a `diff` must split **paths**, not events: a path whose segment
changes inside it would sit in both groups, and the groups would not be
independent. Such a `diff` raises `DiffConfigError` with the count; compare a
segment that is constant per path (an experiment arm attached with
[`add_segment(path_values=...)`](/docs/data-processors/add-segment)) or a finer
`path_col`. Two more things the numbers assume:

- **The denominator is the paths with at least one event.** A user assigned to
  an arm who never produced an event is not counted; for an intention-to-treat
  read that includes them, use `get_ab_test(assignment=...)`.
- **Each path is one independent unit.** That holds when `path_col` is the unit
  that was randomised. Session paths of a user-level experiment are not
  independent and give intervals that are too narrow.

On the bundled A/B test, the purchase path of each arm (the add itself is left
out: in treatment most adds are a different event, `quick_add`):

```python
stream = rete.datasets.load_ab_test()
stream.funnel(
    steps=["product_view", "cart", "checkout_start", "payment", "order_success"],
    diff=("exp_group", "treatment", "control"),
    ci_level=0.95,
    correction="holm",
)
```

The [A/B Testing guide](/docs/ab-testing#funnels-with-intervals) shows this
funnel live and reads its numbers.

Headless, `funnel_data(..., ci_level=0.95)` returns the same numbers: a
`*_ci_low` / `*_ci_high` pair next to every rate and delta,
`delta_conversion_rate_p_value` and `delta_conversion_rate_p_value_adjusted`,
`step_conditional`, and at the top level `ci_level`, `ci_basis`, `correction`
and `correction_family`. Every funnel result also carries its
denominators: `total_paths`, or `funnel1_total_paths` / `funnel2_total_paths`.
An estimate that does not exist (an interval over zero paths, a test where both
groups convert at 0% or 100%) is `NaN`, never 0.

### When the funnel stops being enough

The moment you want to know *why* users dropped between two levels, put the
discarded detail back on a narrower question:

```python
# what actually happens between two levels
stream.truncate_paths(start_anchor="add_to_cart", end_anchor="purchase").transition_graph()

# turn "how far did this path get" into a segment, then compare the groups
labelled = stream.add_segment("funnel", funnel_events=["add_to_cart", "shipping_details", "purchase"])
labelled.step_matrix(diff=("funnel", "shipping_details", "purchase"))
```

See [Path Analysis](/docs/path-analysis) for how a funnel compares with the
step and transition representations of the same paths.

